# Copy to deploy/origin/.env and fill in, then: sudo ./install.sh --check
#
# THE REAL FILE HOLDS SECRETS. .env is gitignored; keep it that way, and never
# paste a tunnel token or a password into an issue, a pull request or a chat
# message. install.sh reads this file and writes /etc/sreoi/*.env from it at
# mode 0640 -- so to rotate anything, edit here and re-run install.sh rather
# than editing the generated files.

# --- Database -------------------------------------------------------------
# Password for the `sreoi` application role. install.sh creates that role
# NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS and applies this password on every run, so changing
# it here and re-running is how you rotate it.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# There is no superuser password here on purpose: install.sh reaches the
# cluster as the local `postgres` OS user over the unix socket to create the
# role and the extensions, so no superuser password needs to exist in a file.
SREOI_DB_PASSWORD=

# --- Public identity ------------------------------------------------------
# The origin the browser uses. Used for CORS. The Pages Function proxies
# /api/* and /auth/* from this same origin, so cross-origin requests are the
# exception rather than the rule -- this is a backstop, not the main path.
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://bahkali-tek.com

# --- Cloudflare Tunnel ----------------------------------------------------
# Zero Trust -> Networks -> Tunnels -> Create a tunnel -> Cloudflared. Copy the
# token out of the install command it shows. It names the tunnel and carries
# its secret, so no credentials file is needed on this host.
#
# This is a credential. If it leaks, delete the tunnel and make a new one.
CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# --- Authentication -------------------------------------------------------
# Choose exactly ONE. install.sh refuses both (the engine would not start) and
# refuses neither (the app would serve nothing).
#
# (a) An identity provider. What this should be for anything real.
# SREOI_OIDC_ISSUER=https://your-idp.example.com/
# SREOI_OIDC_AUDIENCE=sreoi
# SREOI_OIDC_JWKS_URL=https://your-idp.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json
#
# (b) The development password issuer. It signs HS256 tokens with a shared
#     secret and has never been validated against a real OIDC issuer.
#     Acceptable ONLY behind Cloudflare Access, which authenticates every
#     visitor before a request reaches this host at all. Minimum 32 chars:
#       openssl rand -base64 48
SREOI_AUTH_DEV_MODE=1
SREOI_DEV_TOKEN_SECRET=

# The first user and organisation. Leave the password empty to have one
# generated and printed once by install.sh -- it is not recoverable afterwards.
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@bahkali-tek.com
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# 0=silent 1=warn 2=log 3=info 4=debug 5=trace
LOG_LEVEL=2
